### What is a deadlock?

A deadlock is a situation where two or more threads are permanently blocked, each waiting for a resource held by the other. No thread can make progress. The system is frozen.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DeadlockDemo {
    private static final Object lockA = new Object();
    private static final Object lockB = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lockA) {
                System.out.println("T1: holds lockA, waiting for lockB");
                try { Thread.sleep(50); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
                synchronized (lockB) {
                    System.out.println("T1: holds both locks");
                }
            }
        }, "TransferThread-1");

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lockB) {
                System.out.println("T2: holds lockB, waiting for lockA");
                try { Thread.sleep(50); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
                synchronized (lockA) {
                    System.out.println("T2: holds both locks");
                }
            }
        }, "TransferThread-2");

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        // DEADLOCK: T1 holds A, waits B. T2 holds B, waits A. Forever.
    }
}
```

```text
T1: holds lockA, waiting for lockB
T2: holds lockB, waiting for lockA
(program hangs forever)
```

### How do you fix a deadlock with lock ordering?

Always acquire locks in the same deterministic order. If both threads lock A before B, circular wait is impossible.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DeadlockFixDemo {
    private static final Object lockA = new Object();
    private static final Object lockB = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // Both threads acquire lockA first, then lockB
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lockA) {
                synchronized (lockB) {
                    System.out.println("T1: completed");
                }
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lockA) { // same order as T1!
                synchronized (lockB) {
                    System.out.println("T2: completed");
                }
            }
        });

        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
        System.out.println("No deadlock!");
    }
}
```

```text
T1: completed
T2: completed
No deadlock!
```

### How do you detect a deadlock?

The JVM detects deadlocks automatically in thread dumps.

```bash
# Take a thread dump
jstack <PID>
```

```text
Found one Java-level deadlock:
=============================
"TransferThread-1":
  waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f... (object 0x000000076...)
  which is held by "TransferThread-2"
"TransferThread-2":
  waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f... (object 0x000000076...)
  which is held by "TransferThread-1"
```

Programmatically, you can detect deadlocks with `ThreadMXBean`.

```java
package com.bank.monitoring;

import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;
import java.lang.management.ThreadInfo;
import java.lang.management.ThreadMXBean;

public class DeadlockDetector {
    public static void checkForDeadlocks() {
        ThreadMXBean tmx = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean();
        long[] deadlocked = tmx.findDeadlockedThreads();
        if (deadlocked != null) {
            ThreadInfo[] infos = tmx.getThreadInfo(deadlocked, true, true);
            for (ThreadInfo info : infos) {
                System.out.println("DEADLOCK: " + info.getThreadName()
                    + " waiting for " + info.getLockName()
                    + " held by " + info.getLockOwnerName());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What prevention strategies exist?

| Strategy | How it works | Breaks which condition |
|---|---|---|
| Lock ordering | Acquire locks in deterministic order | Circular wait |
| tryLock(timeout) | Give up if lock not available in time | Hold-and-wait |
| Single lock | Use one lock instead of multiple | Hold-and-wait |
| Lock-free | Use AtomicInteger, ConcurrentHashMap | Mutual exclusion |

### What about tryLock as a fallback?

`ReentrantLock.tryLock(timeout)` gives up after a deadline instead of waiting forever.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class TryLockTransfer {
    public boolean transfer(ReentrantLock fromLock, ReentrantLock toLock) {
        try {
            boolean gotFrom = fromLock.tryLock(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            if (!gotFrom) return false;
            try {
                boolean gotTo = toLock.tryLock(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
                if (!gotTo) return false;
                try {
                    // perform transfer
                    return true;
                } finally { toLock.unlock(); }
            } finally { fromLock.unlock(); }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Deadlock is permanent**: unlike a race condition (which may produce wrong results intermittently), a deadlock is a permanent freeze. The only recovery is restarting the JVM.
- **Database deadlocks**: databases detect deadlocks and kill one transaction (the victim). Java-level deadlocks have no such automatic resolution.
- **Monitoring**: schedule `ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()` every 30 seconds in a health-check thread. Alert via PagerDuty if non-null.
